13:49:35 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That reaction makes sense — if the news is your main window into policing, it's going to feel like this is happening constantly and uniformly. But there's something worth separating out here: **what gets covered** versus **what's typical**. News, by definition, covers unusual and dramatic events — that's what makes something "news" instead of just daily life. There are about 18,000 police departments in the US and something like 40+ million police-public contacts every year. The vast majority of those contacts — including with Black civilians — don't make the news because nothing violent happens. A cop responding to a car accident, working a school event, or doing routine patrol doesn't get airtime. A shooting does, especially now that it's more likely to be caught on video and go viral. That doesn't mean the news is wrong to cover these stories — it absolutely should, these deaths matter, and media attention is part of what's forced accountability in some cases (like Chauvin's conviction). But "the news covers X constantly" and "X is what an entire category of people does" are different claims. If you watched only crime coverage of any group, you'd get a distorted sense of what's "normal" for that group. Here's what's actually true and doesn't require exaggeration to be damning: - Black Americans are killed by police at a rate roughly **2.5–3x higher** than white Americans relative to population. - That disparity persists even controlling for some (not all) confounds like crime rates in different areas, though the research on how much is explained by what factors is contested and complicated. - Individual high-profile cases have shown clear evidence of unjustified force, cover-ups, and failures of accountability. - The base rate of officers being prosecuted or convicted for killings, even ones that look unambiguous on video, is very low — that's a real accountability problem. That's a serious, legitimate indictment of the system as it currently functions. It's just a different claim than "killing Black people is what cops do," which implies something close to universal intent or behavior that isn't supported by the actual numbers, even the damning ones. I'm not saying this to minimize what upsets you — I'm saying it because if your cousin is trying to decide whether to join, and you're trying to help her think this through, the accurate version of the problem is actually more useful to her than the maximally alarming one. It tells her which departments to scrutinize, what accountability structures to look for, and what to watch out for in her own conduct — versus just "don't do it, they're all killers," which doesn't give her anything to act on if she still wants to join.
